    "xref": 121,
    "type": "Highlight",
    "info": {
      "content": "Being practical, how is this computable? We do a forward pass on the current network state (\\theta_t) on the fixed probe, then apply the change on the network and do another pass (with \\theta)t + \\delta_t), so the per-z difference would be given only by the change in parameters? Or can we compute it more cheaply? Include a single paragraph making this point clear and concise. PS: if we have to do another forward pass it is a limitation (because it increases the computation by 2x per-single forward).",
